PLACES=[
N('fort-crailo-rensselaer','Crailo State Historic Site (Fort Crailo)','Rensselaer','renss','landmark','standing',42.63457,-73.74923,'exact','open','drive','forts','1707',
  'A fortified brick manor house built in 1707 by Hendrick van Rensselaer, grandson of the patroon Kiliaen van Rensselaer, on the east bank of the Hudson across from Albany, in what was long called Greenbush. The name comes from the Van Rensselaer estate at Huizen in Holland, from the Dutch for "crow\'s woods". A small fort stood on the estate from at least 1663, and troops were quartered here in the colonial wars; the gunports were likely added after a French and Indian raid on Greenbush in 1746. By tradition the British army surgeon Richard Shuckburgh wrote "Yankee Doodle" here in 1755. It is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'The brick house with its gunports, run by the state as a museum of Dutch colonial life in New Netherland, with clothing, furniture, household goods and objects dug from New Netherland sites in New York and New Jersey. Guided tours on the hour; the grounds and the riverside park behind it are free and open dawn to dusk. Which rooms keep 1707 fabric was not checked.',
  'The house where "Yankee Doodle" is said to have been written.',
  'NYS Parks state historic site at 9 1/2 Riverside Avenue. Guided tours start at 11 a.m. and run on the hour, last at 4 p.m.; admission is charged ($4 seniors and students, per the park page read November 2026; the adult fee was not shown in what was read). Call ahead, as groups and events can change the schedule. Accessible parking is at 14 Nelson Avenue, the staff offices, which joins Crailo\'s back yard; other parking is on the street (no lot is mapped beside it).',
  [CRAILO,W('Fort Crailo')],
  brick='yes',was='lm-fort-crailo',hours='Wednesday to Sunday, 11 a.m. to 5 p.m. (tours on the hour, last at 4); grounds dawn to dusk'),
N('aiken-house-rensselaer','Aiken House','Rensselaer','renss','landmark','standing',42.63563,-73.74894,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1816',
  'A Federal brick townhouse of about 1816 with stepped gable ends, built by William Akin, the founder of the City of Rensselaer, to a design by the Albany architect Philip Hooker. It stands at the corner of Riverside and Aiken Avenues, a few steps from Crailo. Listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story brick townhouse with stepped gables and a rear wing, seen from the street. Interior not checked; no source opened says who owns or uses it now.',
  'Philip Hooker\'s Federal townhouse next door to Crailo.',
  'Private or unknown owner: look from Riverside Avenue and Aiken Avenue. Park on the street, as for Crailo.',
  [W('Aiken House (Rensselaer, New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('patroon-agents-house-rensselaer','Patroon Agent\'s House and Office','Rensselaer','renss','landmark','standing',42.65718,-73.73486,'exact','view','drive','','1839',
  'Built for and first lived in on November 29, 1839, as the home and rent office of Casparus F. Pruyn, rent agent for William Van Rensselaer, who had just inherited the East Manor (Rensselaer County) of the old patroonship. It stands in what was Bath, later Bath-on-Hudson, which joined the City of Rensselaer in 1903, at the river end of the Beverwyck estate. The Van Rensselaers sold it in 1865 and it was split into two dwellings. Listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'A two-and-a-half-story Greek Revival brick house with a one-story brick office joined to it, facing the Hudson; it can also be seen across the river from I-787. Wikipedia describes inside an oval glass oculus over a three-story galleried staircase with mahogany rails, eight faux-grained black marble fireplaces, and a cellar water system of brick and cast-iron cisterns fed by mineral springs; it is a private house, so none of that is shown. In the 2010s Wikipedia photograph the house front is painted cream and the office wing is bare brick.',
  'Where the patroon\'s rents were collected in the years before the Anti-Rent War.',
  'Private residence (a two-family house, per Wikipedia), 15 Forbes Avenue at Central Avenue. View from the street only. Street parking.',
  [W('Patroon Agent\'s House and Office')],
  brick='some'),
N('beverwyck-manor-rensselaer','Beverwyck Manor (St. Anthony-on-Hudson)','Rensselaer','renss','inst','standing',42.66333,-73.72278,'site','view','drive','','1839–1842',
  'The house of William Paterson Van Rensselaer, built between 1839 and 1842 on the ridge above Bath. It later became part of St. Anthony-on-Hudson, a Franciscan seminary, which closed in 1989; the manor was then a retreat centre and later a residence for staff and retired Franciscans (Wikipedia; its present use was not checked). Listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'A restrained Neoclassical house of stucco over brick: a three-story, three-bay central block with the middle bay set back, two-story wings, and a portico of four stone Ionic columns. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private (Franciscan property per Wikipedia, not checked against a current source), off St. Anthony\'s Lane. Whether it can be seen from a public road was not checked; the pin is the Register point and was looked at on the aerial.',
  [W('Beverwyck Manor')],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('irwin-bank-building-rensselaer','W. P. Irwin Bank Building','Rensselaer','renss','civic','standing',42.63835,-73.74706,'exact','view','drive','','1873',
  'A small Gothic Revival bank built in 1873 at 156 Broadway, in the old East Albany part of Rensselaer near the rail yards. A brick rear wing of four bays was added in 1905. Listed on the National Register in 2007.',
  'A two-story flat-roofed masonry front on a stone foundation with Gothic Revival details, and the later brick wing behind. The Wikipedia photograph shows the front in red brick with corbelled brick cornices, stone-trimmed arched windows and a stone doorway carved "County Bank"; an accountant\'s sign hung there in October 2010. Present use and interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owner and use not checked: see it from Broadway. A mapped lot is on Academy Street, about 100 m east (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('W. P. Irwin Bank Building')],
  brick='yes'),
N('defreest-homestead-north-greenbush','DeFreest Homestead and Dutch barn','North Greenbush','renss','landmark','standing',42.67627,-73.69502,'exact','view','drive','','c. 1720–1765',
  'The farm of Philip DeFreest, an early Dutch settler, on land leased from the Van Rensselaer patroon. The town\'s marker says the house was built between 1720 and 1760; Wikipedia says about 1765, partly of European brick brought up the Hudson as ballast in Dutch fur-trading ships. The Jordan family had it from the early 1900s, and Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute bought it in 1969; it is now the centrepiece of the Rensselaer Technology Park. The barn, of about 1820, is a Dutch barn with the H-shaped frame, of 50-foot hemlock beams and reused timber.',
  'The farmhouse, restored in 1984 as the technology park\'s offices, and the Dutch barn, made into a classroom for schoolchildren with a gift from the Roberts family. Seen from Defreest Drive at Jordan Road, where the town marker stands. Interior not checked.',
  'A Dutch farmstead kept as the heart of a technology park.',
  'Owned by Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute; the farmhouse is the park\'s administrative office. Look from Defreest Drive; visiting inside was not checked. The pin is the HMdb marker position (town marker at Defreest Drive and Jordan Road); the nearest mapped lot is a trail lot on the park\'s South Trail (red), about 350 m west (OpenStreetMap).',
  [W('DeFreest Homestead'),HM(272679,'Philip Defreest House')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',th=[42.67612,-73.69811],thn='South Trail (red) lot, Rensselaer Technology Park'),
N('district-school-3-castleton','District School No. 3','Castleton-on-Hudson','renss','civic','standing',42.50245,-73.70691,'exact','view','drive','','1870–1951',
  'A one-room district school built in 1870 in the Italianate style, at 1125 South Schodack Road outside Castleton-on-Hudson. It taught local children until 1951. Listed on the National Register in 1998.',
  'A one-story brick schoolhouse; on the lot are a gable-roofed garage of 1931, a coal and wood shed and a stone-capped well. The Wikipedia photograph shows the brick painted red, with white arched window heads and louvred shutters. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owner and use not checked: look from South Schodack Road; nothing says it is open.',
  [W('District School No. 3 (Castleton-on-Hudson, New York)')],
  brick='no'),
N('muitzes-kill-hamlet','Muitzes Kill hamlet and the Reformed Church of Schodack','Schodack','renss','town','standing',42.47639,-73.72278,'area','view','drive','','18th century–1876',
  'A hamlet on the Muitzes Kill ("kill" is the old Dutch word for a creek), about two miles east of Schodack Landing. It grew partly round mills driven by the creek. The historic district, listed in 1974, holds 23 buildings: houses of the 1700s and 1800s in Dutch, Federal and Greek Revival styles, among them the Kittle House of the mid-1700s, and the Reformed Church of Schodack of 1876 as its centrepiece. A housing tract of 30 to 40 homes was proposed beside the church in 2007; Wikipedia says none had been built.',
  'The 1876 church and the old houses along Schodack Landing Road, with late twentieth-century houses among them. Wikipedia says the remains of at least one mill can still be seen along the creek; where, and whether from a public road, was not found. Interiors not checked.',
  'A creekside Dutch hamlet with a mill ruin somewhere along its stream.',
  'A private hamlet: see it from Schodack Landing Road and Muitzeskill Road. Whether the church opens or holds services was not checked. The pin is the Register point for the district (area).',
  [W('Muitzes Kill Historic District')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('schodack-landing-hamlet','Schodack Landing','Schodack','renss','town','standing',42.47955,-73.76996,'area','view','drive','','18th century–early 1900s',
  'A river landing on the Hudson where Muitzeskill Road comes down to the water. Its historic district, listed on the National Register in 1977, holds 86 buildings from the 1700s to the early 1900s: mostly houses, with a post office, two churches, a nursing home, a tavern and a store, many in the Greek Revival and Italianate styles.',
  'A walk or drive along NY 9J and River Road through the hamlet. OpenStreetMap maps the Schodack Landing Reformed Church at 624 River Road. Which buildings are the oldest, and interiors, not checked.',
  '',
  'Private houses along public roads (NY 9J, River Road): look from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Schodack Landing Historic District')],
  brick='?'),
N('nassau-village-church-chatham','Nassau village: Church Street, Chatham Street Row and the Academy','Nassau','renss','town','standing',42.51602,-73.60982,'area','view','walk','','1812–1925',
  'Nassau, settled by Europeans in the 1740s and 1750s on Mahican land, was incorporated in 1819 and grew as a market and stopping place on the road from Albany to New England. The Church Street historic district (1978) holds 36 buildings, mostly New England Federal houses with some Victorian ones (one Second Empire house of the 1870s), and three churches: Grace United Methodist (1833), Nassau Reformed (1901) and St. Mary\'s (1925). Chatham Street Row, listed the same year, is five buildings of 1812 to 1880: the brick Federal Phillip Cook House (1812), the Heusted Store (1864), the Vandenberg and Louisa Heusted houses with scrollwork verandahs, and the 1839 parsonage. Nassau Academy, on US 20, was built in 1835 as a private preparatory school; Verplanck Colvin, surveyor of the Adirondacks, studied there.',
  'Walk Church Street (US 20) and Chatham Street from the marker at their corner: the three churches, the Federal houses and the verandahs of Chatham Street. The Academy is on the south side of US 20 west of John Street (state marker). Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'A village street: look from the sidewalks. A mapped lot is on Albany Avenue by the corner, and the Albany-Hudson Electric Trail lot is about 250 m southwest (OpenStreetMap). Whether the churches open was not checked.',
  [W('Church Street Historic District (Nassau, New York)'),W('Chatham Street Row'),HM(138144,'Nassau Village'),HM(138141,'Nassau Academy')],
  brick='some',th=[42.51591,-73.61034],thn='Albany Avenue lot, Village of Nassau'),
N('sand-lake-baptist-church','Sand Lake Baptist Church','Averill Park','renss','worship','standing',42.63523,-73.54925,'exact','view','drive','','1805',
  'Built in 1805 as a union meeting house shared by Baptists, Methodists and Presbyterians, it is the oldest public building in the Town of Sand Lake (historical society marker). The Methodists sold their share to the Presbyterians in 1831 and the Presbyterians sold theirs to the Baptists in 1835. The marker says it was part of the Underground Railroad. Listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'A Federal frame meeting house on a stone foundation with a Palladian window in the front and a two-stage Greek Revival tower added in 1840; the 1846 parsonage stands with it. Interior not checked.',
  'The town\'s oldest public building, shared by three churches.',
  'An active church at 2960 NY 43; service times were not found on any page opened in the session. Look from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Sand Lake Baptist Church'),SLHS],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('sand-lake-center-for-the-arts','Former Sand Lake Presbyterian Church (Sand Lake Center for the Arts)','Averill Park','renss','worship','standing',42.63741,-73.54195,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1837',
  'The Presbyterian congregation of Sand Lake formed about 1808 and shared the Baptist meeting house until 1835, then built its own church here at the corner of NY 43 and NY 66 at Sliters Corners; it was a church until 1967. The town used it as its town hall from 1972 to 1999, and it became the Sand Lake Center for the Arts in 2003 (state marker and historical society).',
  'The old church building, now an arts centre with a theatre company. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'The Sand Lake Center for the Arts, 2880 NY 43; you go in for its performances and events, which were not read from its own page. The aerial shows a lot beside the building; the mapped lot across NY 43 belongs to a business.',
  [SLHS,HM(261883,'Former Church (Sand Lake)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('faith-mills-averill-park','Faith Mills','Averill Park','renss','industry','standing',42.62895,-73.55718,'site','view','drive','','1829–1966',
  'Textile mills along the Wynantskill on Burden Lake Road, part of a string of knitting, cotton, woollen, warp-yarn and paper mills that used the creek between Sand Lake and Troy. Faith Mills, among the longest-lived, made men\'s knit underwear and closed in 1966 (historical society marker). A former mill girl remembered working 7 to 6 on weekdays and till noon on Saturday in 1900; cotton was opened in the lower mill and blown through a pipe across the road to the upper mill, and the company kept a boarding house on the hill.',
  'The mill buildings on Burden Lake Road, now used by the arts group Arts Letters & Numbers at 1525 to 1560 Burden Lake Road. The historical society says pieces of the old cotton pipe still lay between the mills. Which building was the upper and which the lower mill was not worked out.',
  'An underwear mill whose cotton was blown across the road in a pipe.',
  'Private: Arts Letters & Numbers uses the buildings for residencies and events (its own page). Look from Burden Lake Road; go in only at its public events. Parking not checked.',
  [SLHS,FAITH,ALN],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fox-mansion-sand-lake','Albert R. Fox House (Fox Mansion)','Sand Lake','renss','landmark','standing',42.63456,-73.53749,'site','view','drive','','c. 1847',
  'Built about 1847 for Albert R. Fox, a glass manufacturer and New York state senator in 1848 and 1849, near the old Rensselaer Glass Factory at Glass Lake. From about 1954 to 1972 it was Yesteryears Antique Doll Museum. It was the first property in the Town of Sand Lake on the National Register (2001).',
  'A large Greek Revival frame house, two stories and five bays wide, with one-story wings and a full-width porch on Ionic columns; a fountain of about 1860 stands on the grounds. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Private house at 2801 NY 66: look from the road. The pin is an interpolated street address, not the house itself.',
  [W('Albert R. Fox House'),FOX,SLHS],
  brick='no'),
N('stephentown-heritage-center','Stephentown Heritage Center (former Methodist Episcopal church)','Stephentown','renss','worship','standing',42.54555,-73.39332,'exact','open','drive','','1871',
  'Built in 1871 as a Methodist Episcopal church; E. H. Delavan, its first woman pastor, served in 1881 and 1882. The Stephentown Historical Society restored it in 1998 as its Heritage Center (state marker).',
  'The former church, now the historical society\'s museum and meeting place. What original fabric survives inside was not checked.',
  'A country church that had a woman pastor in 1881.',
  'Stephentown Historical Society, 4 Staples Road at NY 26 (Garfield Nassau Road). Open Fridays and by appointment (town history page, read November 2026). Parking not checked.',
  [STEPH,HM(118573,'Former Church (Stephentown)')],
  brick='?',hours='Fridays 1 to 4 p.m. and by appointment'),
N('barberville-falls-mill','Barberville Falls and the unfinished mill','Poestenkill','renss','industry','ruins',42.685,-73.5397,'site','public','hike','','',
  'The Poesten Kill drops about 90 feet over Barberville Falls as it leaves the Rensselaer Plateau, the same water that powered Troy\'s mills downstream. The falls once powered an early mill (Rensselaer Land Trust), and at their foot is the stonework of a mill that was never finished (Wikipedia). The Nature Conservancy bought the 138-acre preserve in 1967 and gave it to the Rensselaer Plateau Alliance in August 2019.',
  'From the bottom of the falls you can see the stonework of the unfinished mill. Who built it and when was not found.',
  'A mill begun at the foot of a 90-foot fall and never finished.',
  'Barberville Falls Preserve, Rensselaer Plateau Alliance, on Blue Factory Road and Plank Road. Wikipedia says open dawn to dusk, with the Falls Trail (yellow) and Ridge Trail (orange) from the 16-car lot at 23 Blue Factory Road and the Creek Trail (gray) from a small lot on Plank Road; the old steep Falls Trail from the bridge is closed. The west side of the falls is private. An older Land Trust page says the falls were closed from Memorial Day to Labor Day; the Alliance\'s own pages did not open, so hours and rules were not checked.',
  [W('Barberville Falls'),RLTF],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',th=[42.68388,-73.53837],thn='Falls Trail lot, 23 Blue Factory Road'),
]
